<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Lion Sleeps Tonight</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">&quot;Mbube&quot; recorded <a href="/years/1939">1939</a>; &quot;Wimoweh&quot; December <a href="/years/1951">1951</a>; &quot;The Lion Sleeps Tonight&quot; October <a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The song begins with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbube">Mbube</a>&quot;, written by the South African singer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-linda">Solomon Linda</a>. Born in rural Natal, Linda was a migrant worker and beer-hall singer who led an a cappella group, the Evening Birds, and worked as a packer at Eric Gallo's record-pressing plant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>. At their second session in Gallo's studio, in 1939, the Evening Birds recorded &quot;Mbube&quot; in three takes without rehearsal.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbube">Mbube</a>&quot; was written and composed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-linda">Solomon Linda</a> in 1939. It was first published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> a decade later. It is sung in a call and response format in four-part harmony, over three chords in a progression borrowed from the marabi harmonic cycle common in twentieth-century South African music. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> lyrics are said to document an episode of Linda's childhood when he chased a lion while herding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. In the closing seconds Linda breaks into a brief howl, which Rian Malan describes as a haunting skein of fifteen notes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Gallo sent records to Decca in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-lomax">Alan Lomax</a> salvaged them from being discarded and handed the box to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> of the Weavers. Seeger transcribed the song word for word but misheard the chorus as &quot;wimoweh&quot;. The Weavers recorded it as &quot;Wimoweh&quot; in 1951 at the Village Vanguard, with Seeger singing falsetto, and Gordon Jenkins added a brass accompaniment that stressed Linda's howl. Malan wrote that it was faithful to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> original in almost every respect except for the finger-popping rhythm.</p><p>In 1961 the Brooklyn doo-wop group the Tokens, who had heard the Weavers live, recorded their own version for RCA. They changed the melody and added English lyrics, and it topped the US charts. By the mid-2000s over 150 artists had recorded the song. Robert John took it to No. 3 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1971, and Tight Fit topped the British charts in 1982. The song was featured in Disney's The Lion King in 1994.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Disputes over credit began about 1951, when Seeger protested that Linda, as the true author, should be paid, and at one point sent him a cheque of $1,000 himself. In 1971 The Richmond Organization acknowledged that the song was based on &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbube">Mbube</a>&quot;, and from then on Linda's family received 12.5 percent of the earnings of &quot;Wimoweh&quot;. The Tokens' writers, Hugo Peretti, Luigi Creatore and George David Weiss, wrongly thought the tune traditional and credited their version to themselves.</p><p>Malan's essay in Rolling Stone in 2000 estimated that the song had earned some $15 million in royalties, and François Verster's documentary A Lion's Trail, two years later, drew further attention. In July 2004 Linda's family sued Disney for 10 million rand over The Lion King, with the support of the South African government. Within two years they reached an out-of-court settlement with Abilene Music for past royalties and a share of future revenue. The case had legal effects, including on British copyright law. Some writers call &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbube">Mbube</a>&quot; the most widely known melody born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Lion Sleeps Tonight” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Released in October 1961 as a B-side, &quot;<b>The Lion Sleeps Tonight</b>&quot; reached No.</blockquote><a href="/songs/mbube">Mbube</a></li><li><blockquote>Produced by Hugo Peretti and Luigi Creatore, &quot;<b>The Lion Sleeps Tonight</b>&quot; came out in October 1961 as a B-side and rose to No.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-lion-sleeps-tonight">The Lion Sleeps Tonight</a></li></ul></section>
